    53→\section{Introduction}
    54→
    55→This template is aligned with the good practices stated by \citet{EditorialGRSL2015}, which are a step towards a reproducible article \citep{ABadgingSystemforReproducibilityandReplicabilityinRemoteSensingResearch}.
    56→We used the official IEEE Conference Proceedings template available here \url{https://www.ieee.org/conferences/publishing/templates.html} to produce this simplified version for IGARSS~2025.
    57→This template uses \BibTeX, a convenient way of managing references that simplifies the formatting and automatically selects the bibliographic entries cited in the text.
    58→
    59→A basic knowledge of \LaTeX\ and \BibTeX\ is required.
    60→
    61→\section{Document Structure}
    62→
    63→Conceptually, a scientific document should have four elements:
    64→\begin{itemize}
    65→	\item Introduction: your scientific question, the review of the literature, an outline of the steps you took to answer, a summary of what you observed, and a brief statement of how those observations changed the state-of-the-art (that was outlined in the review of the literature).
    66→	\item Methodology: describe what you did so other researchers can replicate your study.
    67→	\item Results: the core observations you collected, usually summarized in tables, figures, and images.
    68→	\item Discussion: this is the essential part of your work. Do not summarize your results. Tell the reader how they answer (or not) your scientific question and how they change the state-of-the-art.
    69→\end{itemize}
    70→This structure is known as ``IMRaD,'' and was consolidated by over a century of scientific practice.
    71→Fig.~\ref{fig:IMRAD} shows a quote from the book by \citet{Day1998} about this structure, and this piece of code shows how to include a figure in your article.
    72→The file is in PNG format; reserve this format for matrix-like graphical elements.
    73→Use PDF for plots, as in Fig.~\ref{fig:Densities}.
    74→
    75→\begin{figure}[hbt]
    76→	\centering
    77→	\includegraphics[width=.9\linewidth]{Day-IMRaD}
    78→	\caption{A screenshot from \citet{Day1998} about the structure of a scientific article.}\label{fig:IMRAD}
    79→\end{figure}
    80→
    81→The main textual components are controlled by the commands \verb|\section|, \verb|\subsection|, and \verb|\subsubsection|.
    82→
    83→\subsection{This is a subsection}
    84→
    85→Text components are automatically numbered.
    86→
    87→\subsubsection{This is a subsection inside a subsection} This is the innermost text component you should use in your document. 
    88→It appears as a paragraph with a title.
    89→
    90→\subsubsection{Nice tables} Citing \citet{simplexCT}:
    91→\begin{quote}
    92→	Tables are best suited for looking up precise values, comparing individual values or presenting values involving multiple units of measure. Graphs, on the other hand, are better for detecting trends, anomalies or relations. In other words, graphs show the forest while tables show the trees.
    93→\end{quote}
    94→
    95→Once you have designed your table, type it using commands from \verb|booktabs|, as in Table~\ref{tab:MyTable}.
    96→Notice that the second column type was stipulated with commands from the \verb|siunitx| package.
    97→It switches to mathematical mode and aligns the figures by their decimal point, promoting an immediate visual comparison.
    98→
    99→\begin{table}[hbt]
   100→	\centering
   101→	\caption{True and estimated classes, with the sample mean.}\label{tab:MyTable}
   102→	\begin{tabular}{r S[table-format=3.2] l}
   103→		\toprule
   104→		\textbf{True LULC} & \textbf{Mean} & \textbf{Estimated LULC} \\ \cmidrule(lr){1-1} \cmidrule(lr){2-2}\cmidrule(lr){3-3}
   105→		Sand & 1.32 & Open sea\\
   106→		Forest & 7.93 & Forest\\
   107→		Open sea & -5 & Open sea\\
   108→		Bare soil & 100.41 & Bare soil\\ \bottomrule
   109→	\end{tabular}
   110→\end{table}
   111→
   112→Tables and algorithms captions are usually at the top,
   113→while figures captions are at the bottom.
   114→
   115→\subsection{Another subsection}
   116→
   117→Your document should have at least two components for each level, i.e., do not use a single \verb|\subsubsection|, but at least two.
   118→
   119→One of \LaTeX's strongest abilities is producing high-quality equations:
   120→\begin{equation}
   121→	i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \Psi(\bm{r}, t) = \widehat{H} \Psi(\bm{r}, t),
   122→\end{equation}
   123→where $i=\sqrt{-1}$ is the imaginary unit,
   124→$\hbar$ is the reduced Plank's constant,
   125→${\partial}/{\partial t}$ is the partial derivative operator with respect to time,
   126→$\Psi(\bm{r}, t)$ is the wave equation that depends on the position $\bm r$ and time $t$, and
   127→$\widehat{H}$ is the Hamiltonian operator.
   128→Always detail every component of each equation.
   129→
   130→Use \verb|amsmath|'s mathematical environments:
   131→\begin{equation}
   132→	f_{\text{tr} A}(x) =
   133→	\Big| \frac{x\beta}{2} \Sigma^{-1} \Big|^a
   134→	\frac{e^{-z}}{x\Gamma(ma)} {}_1F_x^{(2a)} 
   135→	\Big(a;ma;z I_m - \frac{x\beta}{2}\Sigma^{-1}\Big),
   136→\end{equation}
   137→in which we have followed the notation defined by \citet{TheDensitiesandDistributionsoftheLargestEigenvalueandtheTraceofaBetaWishartMatrix}.
   138→The package offers several options to align equations.
   139→Often, an equation is wider than a column; in that case, use the \verb|\begin{multline} ... \end{multline}| environment.
   140→\begin{multline}
   141→	\Pr\big(\text{tr} A \leq (x)\big) =
   142→	\Big| \frac{x\beta}{2} \Sigma^{-1} \Big|^a
   143→	\frac{1}{\Gamma(ma+1)} \\ 
   144→	{}_1F_x^{(2a)} \Big(a;ma+1;- \frac{x\beta}{2}\Sigma^{-1}\Big).
   145→\end{multline}
